hey I have the same problem and I hope this will be fixed, a simple example
```
const appRoutes: Routes = [
...
  {
    path: 'tasks',
    loadChildren: () =>
      import('./pages/tasks/tasks.module').then(m => m.TasksPageModule),
  },
];
```
```
@NgModule({
  imports: [
    CommonModule,
    RouterModule.forChild([
      {
        path: '',
        component: TasksPage,
        resolve: {
          tasks: TasksResolver,
        },
        runGuardsAndResolvers: 'always',
        canActivate: [AuthGuardService],
      },
      {
        path: ':id',
        component: TaskPage,
        resolve: {
          tasks: TaskResolver,
        },
        runGuardsAndResolvers: 'always',
        canActivate: [AuthGuardService],
      },
    ]),
  ],
  declarations: [TasksPage],
  providers: [TasksResolver],
})
export class TasksPageModule {}
```
when I navigate to /tasks and TasksResolver will fail and return empty() the navigation is not cancelled

the only ways to get that working is:
```
// move that
  {
        path: 'tasks',
        component: TasksPage,
        resolve: {
          tasks: TasksResolver,
        },
// to the app routes, but then lazy loading will load the whole module
// and it's not that lazy anymore
const appRoutes: Routes = [
...
  {
        path: 'tasks',
        component: TasksPage,
        resolve: {
          tasks: TasksResolver,
        },
        runGuardsAndResolvers: 'always',
        canActivate: [AuthGuardService],
        loadChildren: () =>
          import('./pages/tasks/tasks.module').then(m => m.TasksPageModule),
      },
];
```
or manually navigate to previous route in your resolver 
```
this.router.navigate(['/']);
```

I had a similar issue with resolvers today. 
I tried to stop navigation by returning EMPTY in resolver, though a catchError in the observable. 

```
 return this.webservice.getThings().pipe(
            catchError((err, caught) => {
                console.log('ERROR');
                console.log(err);
                this.started = false;
                if (this.spinner) {
                    this.loadingController.dismiss();
                }
                return EMPTY;
            }),
            tap({
                next: () => { this.started = false; if (this.spinner) { this.spinner.dismiss(); } },
                error: () => { this.started = false; if (this.spinner) { this.spinner.dismiss(); } },
            })
        );
```

Returning EMPTY stops navigation, but triggers an "EmptyError: no elements in sequence" error. 
May be related to #24621.
